window.docContents[83].resolve({"/#Functional-Programming-in-Lean--Monads--Summary--The-Monad-Type-Class":{"contents":"It's possible to write purely functional programs in languages that allow effects anywhere.\nFor example, 2 + 3 is a valid Python program that has no effects at all.\nSimilarly, combining programs that have effects requires a way to state the order in which the effects must occur.\nIt matters whether an exception is thrown before or after modifying a variable, after all.\n\nThe type class  captures these two important properties.\nIt has two methods:  represents programs that have no effects, and  sequences effectful programs.\nThe contract for  instances ensures that  and  actually capture pure computation and sequencing.\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Monads\u0009Summary","header":"4.7.2. The Monad Type Class","id":"/#Functional-Programming-in-Lean--Monads--Summary--The-Monad-Type-Class"},"/#Functional-Programming-in-Lean--Overloading-and-Type-Classes--Arrays-and-Indexing":{"contents":"The Interlude describes how to use indexing notation in order to look up entries in a list by their position.\nThis syntax is also governed by a type class, and it can be used for a variety of different types.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Overloading and Type Classes","header":"3.4. Arrays and Indexing","id":"/#Functional-Programming-in-Lean--Overloading-and-Type-Classes--Arrays-and-Indexing"},"/#out-params-exercises":{"contents":"Define an instance of  that multiplies both projections by the scalar.\nIt should work for any type  for which there is a  instance.\nFor example,\n\n\n\nshould yield\n\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Overloading and Type Classes\u0009Controlling Instance Search","header":"3.3.4. Exercises","id":"/#out-params-exercises"}});